{{ name }} started from [labinacan-template](https://github.com/8W9aG/labinacan-template). Its site, papers, posts, literature review and brand come from the [labinacan](https://pypi.org/project/labinacan/) package, which it installs from PyPI. Its own code lives in `{{ package }}/`.

## Develop

```bash
make install    # the locked dependencies, with the dev group
make lint       # the checks of code and prose
make test
make build      # the site, in dist/
make serve
```

## First steps

`copier copy` set up this project from its answers, which `.copier-answers.yml` keeps. Its task drew the brand and, if you asked, set up the GitHub repository with `gh`. It printed what it couldn't do. Run it again at any time with `uv run python scripts/setup_repo.py`.

1. **Commit the lock.** The task wrote `uv.lock`. CI installs from it.
2. **Set the secrets** that the task listed, with `gh secret set <NAME>`.
3. **Write the project's part of each skill.** A skill that has a `project.md` beside its `SKILL.md` reads it first. The release's own steps, the breaking surfaces and the priorities of issues are examples.
4. **Remove the example's content:** the sample paper in `papers/`, the post in `posts/` and the review in `docs/literature/`. Write your own.

`scripts/check_leftovers.py` finds words of another project in the files. Put the words of the project that you came from in `scripts/leftover_terms.txt`.

## Take the template's changes

```bash
uvx copier update --trust
```

copier merges each later change of the template into this project, and keeps the project's own changes. It shows a conflict as one in Git. A new release of `labinacan` comes in the same way, as a new pin in `pyproject.toml`.

## The config

`[tool.labinacan]` in `pyproject.toml` holds the project's values:

| Key | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | the project's name as people read it |
| `package` | its Python package, which holds `__version__` and the brand |
| `env_prefix` | the prefix of its environment variables |
| `linear_team` | the Linear team that mirrors its issues |
| `maintainer` | the maintainer's GitHub login, whose issues agents may work |
| `hf_dataset` | the Hugging Face dataset of the inputs that its papers cite |
| `generated` | the files that a tool writes, which the checks of hand-written files skip |
| `data_only` | the folders whose changes alone run no lint and tests |
| `automatic_issue_workflows` | the workflows whose failure issues an agent may work |

`auto_merge_rule` is optional. It names a function, as `"path.py:function"`, that takes one file's name. A pull request from anyone that only adds files that the function accepts merges by itself once CI passes. With no rule, only the maintainer's and Dependabot's pull requests merge by themselves (`.github/workflows/auto-merge.yml`).

`roadmap_project` is optional. It is the number of the maintainer's GitHub project that is the Roadmap, which `scripts/linear_sync.py` keeps in step with Linear when `ROADMAP_TOKEN` is set. With no number, the sync skips the Roadmap.

`[tool.labinacan.brand]` draws the mark, the logo and the social card, and gives the site's accent. After a change, draw them again with `uv run python scripts/brand_images.py`. `[tool.labinacan.size_budget]`, `[tool.labinacan.exchange]` and `[tool.labinacan.breaking]` are optional.

## Layout

| Path | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `{{ package }}/` | the project's own code, and its brand |
| `labinacan.mk` | the Make targets that the `Makefile` includes |
| `scripts/` | the checks of code and prose, the changelog, mutation testing, the issue loop and Linear sync |
| `.claude/skills/` | the skills that agents follow |
| `.github/workflows/` | CI, the secret scan, the link check, auto-merge, the deploy, publishing and issue syncs |

The deploy (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`) builds the site on each push to `main`. It syncs the site to Cloudflare R2 and posts new papers to X only when the repository variable `R2_BUCKET` is set. The project's own steps before the build go in a Make target that `DEPLOY_PREPARE` names.
| `papers/`, `posts/`, `docs/literature/` | the project's content |

